    "result_text": "The specification is satisfied and the test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe requirement was that all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body carrying `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`.\n\n**What I found:** Every 400/409/429 rejection in this app is raised as a domain exception (`InvalidTelephoneException`, `InvalidEmailException`, `InvalidAddressException`, `InvalidPostcodeException`, `FutureRegistrationDateException` \u2192 400; `DuplicateIdentityException`, `CityCapacityExceededException` \u2192 409; `DailyOwnerLimitExceededException` \u2192 429), plus `MethodArgumentNotValidException` for bean-validation 400s. All of these are handled centrally in `ExceptionControllerAdvice`, whose `detailBuild(...)` constructs a Spring `ProblemDetail` and returns it via `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>`:\n\n- `type` \u2014 set to the request URL (`problemDetail.setType(...)`)\n- `title` \u2014 the exception's simple class name\n- `status` \u2014 via `ProblemDetail.forStatus(status)`\n- `detail` \u2014 the human-readable message\n\nBecause the body is a `ProblemDetail`, Spring MVC serializes it as `application/problem+json` and the HTTP status is unchanged. There are no rejection paths that bypass this advice (e.g., the idempotency store never produces a 409).\n\n**Verification:**\n- The provided `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectionIsProblemJson` (400, content type + `$.status`) passes.\n- I added a temporary test to empirically confirm the 409 (duplicate identity) and 429 (daily-limit) paths also emit `application/problem+json` with all four members present \u2014 both passed \u2014 then removed it.\n- Full suite: **BUILD SUCCESS**, all tests green.\n\nNo source changes were needed \u2014 the existing `ExceptionControllerAdvice` already fully implements the RFC7807 contract for all three rejection statuses.",
